        {
            "name": "[Lytbox] Client Onboarding SOP",
            "content": "<h1>Client Onboarding SOP</h1>\n<p>From Signed Contract → Project Kickoff</p>\n<h3>Purpose</h3>\n<p>This SOP ensures every new client is onboarded in a structured, consistent way before design begins.</p>\n<p>This process begins only after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contract is signed</li>\n<li>Deposit is received</li>\n<li>The objective is to move the client from “closed deal” to “ready for project kickoff” with clarity and organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 1 — Confirm &amp; Initiate</h2>\n<h3>1. Confirm Agreement &amp; Payment</h3>\n<p>Before any onboarding begins, confirm:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contract signed</li>\n<li>Deposit received</li>\n<li>Scope finalized</li>\n<li>Do not proceed without both.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Duplicate the Client Hub (Initial Setup)</h3>\n<p>Immediately after deposit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Duplicate the Client Hub template</li>\n<li>Rename it (Client Name – Project Name – Year)</li>\n<li>Update basic project details</li>\n<li>Add primary contact and decision maker</li>\n<li>Update Scope &amp; Deliverables (based on proposal)</li>\n<li>Customize initial Asset Checklist</li>\n<li>Ensure comments are enabled</li>\n<li>Share access with client</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important:</p>\n<p>At this stage, the Client Hub is a structured shell. The Project Brief and strategy details will be completed after discovery.</p>\n<h3>3. Send Welcome Email</h3>\n<p>Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Welcome message</li>\n<li>Link to Client Hub</li>\n<li>Instructions on how to use it</li>\n<li>Asset submission instructions</li>\n<li>Clear deadline for required assets</li>\n<li>Clarify that timeline begins once required assets are received</li>\n<li>Log onboarding date internally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 2 — Discovery</h2>\n<p>Select the appropriate path.</p>\n<h3>Option A — Discovery Call (High-Level or Basic)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Schedule discovery call</li>\n<li>Facilitate session</li>\n<li>Capture detailed notes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Identify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary goal</li>\n<li>Core problem to solve</li>\n<li>Target audience</li>\n<li>Success definition</li>\n<li>Summarize findings inside Client Hub under Project Brief</li>\n<li>Confirm alignment with client</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Option B — Discovery Questionnaire</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Send discovery questionnaire</li>\n<li>Review responses</li>\n<li>Clarify vague or incomplete answers</li>\n<li>Summarize findings inside Client Hub</li>\n<li>Confirm project direction if necessary</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After discovery is complete:</p>\n<p>Update the Client Hub with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Target audience summary</li>\n<li>Core business goal</li>\n<li>Problems to solve</li>\n<li>Definition of success</li>\n<li>The Client Hub is now fully populated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 3 — Asset Collection</h2>\n<p>The Asset Collection System lives inside the Client Hub.</p>\n<h3>1. Customize Asset Checklist</h3>\n<p>Review the project and confirm required assets.</p>\n<p>Update the checklist to reflect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brand assets needed</li>\n<li>Content requirements</li>\n<li>Image requirements</li>\n<li>Legal requirements</li>\n<li>Access credentials</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Collect Minimum Required Assets</h3>\n<p>Design cannot begin until minimum assets are received.</p>\n<p>Minimum required:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Logo (vector preferred)</li>\n<li>Core website content</li>\n<li>Brand direction</li>\n<li>Required technical access</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Store &amp; Organize</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Upload all files inside “Files &amp; Uploads” section</li>\n<li>OR</li>\n<li>Link Google Drive folder</li>\n<li>Everything must be centralized inside the Client Hub.</li>\n<li>Update checkboxes as assets are received.</li>\n<li>Follow up on missing items as needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 4 — Access Verification</h2>\n<p>Before kickoff, confirm access:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Domain registrar access</li>\n<li>DNS access</li>\n<li>Hosting login</li>\n<li>CMS login (if redesign)</li>\n<li>Required integrations access</li>\n<li>Test credentials where possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 5 — Final Validation</h2>\n<p>Before moving to project management, confirm:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discovery complete</li>\n<li>Client Hub fully updated</li>\n<li>Scope confirmed</li>\n<li>Deliverables confirmed</li>\n<li>Minimum assets received</li>\n<li>Access verified</li>\n<li>Communication expectations clear</li>\n<li>If anything is incomplete, onboarding is not finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PHASE 6 — Transition to Project Management</h2>\n<p>Once validated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activate Project Management board</li>\n<li>Move project to “Kickoff” stage</li>\n<li>Notify client that design phase is starting</li>\n<li>Client onboarding is now complete.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>VA HANDOFF CHECKLIST</h2>\n<p>This checklist can be delegated to a VA or assistant.</p>\n<h3>Admin</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm contract signed</li>\n<li>Confirm deposit received</li>\n<li>Create project folder (if using Drive)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Notion Setup</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Duplicate Client Hub</li>\n<li>Rename correctly</li>\n<li>Update basic project details</li>\n<li>Update Scope &amp; Deliverables</li>\n<li>Customize Asset Checklist</li>\n<li>Enable comments</li>\n<li>Share with client</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Communication</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Send welcome email</li>\n<li>Include Client Hub link</li>\n<li>Set asset deadline</li>\n<li>Log onboarding date</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Discovery</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Schedule discovery call</li>\n<li>OR</li>\n<li>Send discovery questionnaire</li>\n<li>Store responses</li>\n<li>Notify project lead when discovery complete</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Asset Collection</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Follow up on missing assets</li>\n<li>Update asset checklist</li>\n<li>Store files in Files &amp; Uploads section</li>\n<li>Confirm minimum assets complete</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Access</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm domain access</li>\n<li>Confirm DNS access</li>\n<li>Confirm hosting login</li>\n<li>Confirm CMS login</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ready for Kickoff</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Discovery complete</li>\n<li>Client Hub finalized</li>\n<li>Assets complete</li>\n<li>Access verified</li>\n<li>Notify project lead: “Ready for Kickoff”</li>\n</ul>"
        },
        {
            "name": "[Lytbox] Client Discovery Questionnaire",
            "content": "<h1>Client Discovery Questionnaire</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast-Track Version (For Lower Budget Projects)</li>\n<li>Use this in Notion or Google Forms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SECTION 1 — The Business</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In one or two sentences, what does your business do?</li>\n<li>What primary service or product should this website focus on?</li>\n<li>What is the main goal of this website?</li>\n<li>Generate leads</li>\n<li>Book appointments</li>\n<li>Sell products</li>\n<li>Build credibility</li>\n<li>Other (please specify)</li>\n<li>What problem do you want this website to solve?</li>\n<li>Where are most of your customers currently coming from?</li>\n<li>Social media</li>\n<li>Referrals</li>\n<li>Search engines</li>\n<li>Paid ads</li>\n<li>Other</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SECTION 2 — Your Brand</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>How would you describe your brand in 3 to 5 words?</li>\n<li>How should customers feel when visiting your website?</li>\n<li>Do you already have brand assets ready?</li>\n<li>Logo</li>\n<li>Brand colors</li>\n<li>Fonts</li>\n<li>Brand guide</li>\n<li>None yet</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SECTION 3 — Your Ideal Customer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Who is your ideal customer?</li>\n<li>What problem are they trying to solve?</li>\n<li>Why should they choose you instead of competitors?</li>\n<li>What would instantly build trust with them?</li>\n<li>Testimonials</li>\n<li>Certifications</li>\n<li>Portfolio</li>\n<li>Before and after results</li>\n<li>Clear pricing</li>\n<li>Other</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SECTION 4 — Competitors &amp; Inspiration</h3>\n<p>Are there any competitor websites you like or even envy? Please share links.</p>\n<p>Are there any websites you like (inside or outside your industry)? What do you like about them?</p>\n<h3>SECTION 5 — Current Website (If Applicable)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Do you currently have a website?</li>\n<li>Yes</li>\n<li>No</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If yes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is not working?</li>\n<li>What do you want to improve?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SECTION 6 — Project Logistics &amp; Onboarding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Who will be the main point of contact for this project?</li>\n<li>Who is the final decision maker?</li>\n<li>What is your preferred method of communication?</li>\n<li>Email</li>\n<li>Slack</li>\n<li>WhatsApp</li>\n<li>Other</li>\n<li>Do you have a preferred launch date?</li>\n<li>Will you be providing the website content and images?</li>\n<li>Yes</li>\n<li>No</li>\n<li>Partially</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If no or partially:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Would you like assistance with copywriting or sourcing images?</li>\n<li>Who will manage the website after launch?</li>\n<li>You</li>\n<li>Your team</li>\n<li>Need help with this</li>\n</ul>"
        },
        {
            "name": "[Lytbox] Basic Discovery Exercise Framework",
            "content": "<h1>Basic Discovery Exercise Framework</h1>\n<p>(30–45 Minutes | Alignment + Onboarding)</p>\n<h3>Goals for Discovery</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify project goals and problems to solve</li>\n<li>Identify target customer</li>\n<li>Clarify brand direction</li>\n<li>Set expectations and begin onboarding</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>OPENING</h2>\n<p>“The goal of this call is to clarify your objectives, target audience, and expectations so we can move forward with a focused plan. We want this to be more than just a good-looking website. We want it to support your business goals.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>We’ll also cover onboarding and next steps so everything is clear from the start.</li>\n<li>This call should take around 30–45 minutes.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>STRUCTURE</h2>\n<h3>PART 1 — The Business</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep this tight.</li>\n<li>Tell me about your business in simple terms.</li>\n<li>What primary service or product should this website focus on?</li>\n<li>What problem do you want this website to solve?</li>\n<li>Where are most of your customers currently coming from?</li>\n<li>If we improve one thing, what should it be?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 2 — The Brand</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>How would you describe your brand?</li>\n<li>What 3–5 words best describe it?</li>\n<li>How should customers feel when they visit your website?</li>\n<li>Do you have brand guidelines, colors, fonts, or assets ready?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 3 — Target Customer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Who is your ideal customer?</li>\n<li>What problem are they trying to solve?</li>\n<li>Why should they choose you?</li>\n<li>What would immediately build trust with them?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Short. Focused. No deep persona build.</p>\n<h3>PART 4 — Competitors &amp; Inspiration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Are there any competitor websites you like or even envy?</li>\n<li>Are there any websites (inside or outside your industry) you like?</li>\n<li>What specifically do you like about them?</li>\n<li>This helps design direction quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 5 — Current Website (If Applicable)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What is not working with your current website?</li>\n<li>What do you want to keep or improve?</li>\n<li>What should we avoid repeating?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 6 — Defining Success</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep this foundational.</li>\n<li>What would make this project successful in your eyes?</li>\n<li>Is there a measurable goal? (Leads, bookings, inquiries)</li>\n<li>What does a smooth working relationship look like to you?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 7 — Setting Expectations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>This stays strong even in basic projects.</li>\n<li>Recap scope and deliverables.</li>\n<li>Explain design → revision → development flow.</li>\n<li>Emphasize importance of content and feedback timing.</li>\n<li>Clarify communication method and update frequency.</li>\n<li>Explain how scope changes are handled.</li>\n<li>Confirm timeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 8 — Onboarding Alignment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Preferred communication method?</li>\n<li>Who is final decision-maker?</li>\n<li>Any upcoming travel or limited availability?</li>\n<li>Who is providing content and images?</li>\n<li>Who will manage the site after launch? (ignore if client is going on our maintenance plan)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 9 — Recap</h3>\n<p>You summarize:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main business goal</li>\n<li>Core problem</li>\n<li>Target customer</li>\n<li>Success definition</li>\n<li>Timeline + next step</li>\n<li>Confirm alignment.</li>\n<li>Done.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Key Difference Between High-Level and Basic</h2>\n<p>High-Level:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue discussion</li>\n<li>Growth modeling</li>\n<li>Persona depth</li>\n<li>Strategic positioning</li>\n<li>Often paid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Basic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clarity + alignment</li>\n<li>Faster execution</li>\n<li>Less strategic modeling</li>\n<li>Still professional onboarding</li>\n</ul>"
        },
        {
            "name": "[Lytbox] High Level Discovery Exercise Framework",
            "content": "<h1>High-Level Discovery Exercise Framework</h1>\n<p>(45–90 Minutes | Strategic Alignment + Onboarding)</p>\n<h3>Goals for Discovery</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify project goals, success metrics, and real problems to solve</li>\n<li>Identify target customer and buying behavior</li>\n<li>Clarify brand positioning, tone, and messaging direction</li>\n<li>Set expectations and complete onboarding</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>OPENING SCRIPT (Facilitator Frame)</h2>\n<p>“The goal of this call is to identify your business objectives, challenges, and target customers so we can approach your project with clarity and measurable outcomes. We want this to be more than just a website that looks good. We want it to support your business goals and speak directly to your customers.</p>\n<p>We’ll also go through the onboarding process today so expectations are clear and there are no surprises.</p>\n<p>I estimate this call will take 60–90 minutes. I’ll guide the conversation and ask detailed questions. Some may feel repetitive, but each serves a strategic purpose. Please feel free to ask questions at any time.”</p>\n<p>You lead the call. Not them.</p>\n<h2>STRUCTURE</h2>\n<h3>PART 1 — The Business (Strategy First)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Purpose: Identify revenue drivers, problems, and opportunity.</li>\n<li>Tell me about your business in simple terms.</li>\n<li>How does your business generate revenue?</li>\n<li>What are your primary sales channels?</li>\n<li>Where are most of your customers coming from right now?</li>\n<li>What is working well in your business today?</li>\n<li>What is not working?</li>\n<li>What problem do you believe this website needs to solve?</li>\n<li>If we could make the biggest impact in one area, where would that be?</li>\n<li>What is a realistic growth goal for the next 12 months?</li>\n<li>If this project succeeds, what financial or operational impact would that have?</li>\n<li>We are diagnosing business opportunity, not just discussing design.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 2 — The Brand</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Purpose: Clarify positioning and emotional direction.</li>\n<li>How would you describe your brand positioning?</li>\n<li>What makes you different from competitors?</li>\n<li>What 3–5 words describe your brand best?</li>\n<li>How do you want customers to feel when interacting with your brand?</li>\n<li>What impression should they leave with after visiting your website?</li>\n<li>Do you have brand guidelines, fonts, color systems, or voice standards?</li>\n<li>Are there any competitors websites you like or even envy?</li>\n<li>Capture brand adjectives clearly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 3 — The Target Customer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Purpose: Define who we are designing and messaging for.</li>\n<li>Who is your ideal customer?</li>\n<li>Why do they buy from you?</li>\n<li>What problem are they trying to solve?</li>\n<li>What objections or hesitations do they typically have?</li>\n<li>Why do they choose you over competitors?</li>\n<li>What type of customer would you like more of?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 4 — Focused Persona Exercise (Strategic Version)</h3>\n<p>Explain: “We build 1–3 focused personas to ensure messaging is targeted, not generic.”</p>\n<p>Questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Describe your #1 ideal customer in detail.</li>\n<li>What motivates them emotionally?</li>\n<li>What frustrates them?</li>\n<li>What outcome are they seeking?</li>\n<li>What objections do they need overcome?</li>\n<li>Where do they research before buying?</li>\n<li>What would immediately build trust with them?</li>\n<li>What would cause them to leave your website?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Optional:</p>\n<p>9. What percentage of revenue do they represent?</p>\n<p>Name the persona if helpful.</p>\n<p>Keep it strategic. Not fictional storytelling.</p>\n<h3>PART 5 — Current Website Review</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Purpose: Avoid repeat mistakes.</li>\n<li>What were the biggest frustrations with your previous website?</li>\n<li>What worked well?</li>\n<li>What did not?</li>\n<li>What feedback did customers give?</li>\n<li>How was your experience with previous designers or developers?</li>\n<li>What should we absolutely avoid this time?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 6 — Defining Success</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Foundational.</li>\n<li>What would make this project a success in your eyes?</li>\n<li>How would we measure success? (Leads, conversions, revenue, engagement)</li>\n<li>What does a successful brand presentation look like?</li>\n<li>What does a successful working relationship look like?</li>\n<li>This sets alignment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 7 — Setting Expectations</h3>\n<p>This is where professionals separate themselves. This is also a step that prevent scope creep, set timelines, and ensure a smooth project for everyone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recap scope and deliverables.</li>\n<li>Clarify phases: Discovery → Design → Development → Launch.</li>\n<li>Explain revision structure.</li>\n<li>Explain that changes after approval may affect scope and budget.</li>\n<li>Emphasize importance of timely content and feedback.</li>\n<li>Define communication rhythm (for example: do you prefer emails, slack, whatsapp, or video calls and how often?).</li>\n<li>Clarify approval authority (who signs off).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pause. Invite questions.</p>\n<h3>PART 8 — Onboarding Alignment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep simple and clear.</li>\n<li>Preferred communication method?</li>\n<li>Preferred frequency of updates? (for example: how often would you like check-ins and updates?).</li>\n<li>Any upcoming travel or limited availability?</li>\n<li>Who must be included in approvals?</li>\n<li>Who will manage the website after launch? (if not on our maintenance plans)</li>\n<li>Are we creating content or is the client providing it?</li>\n<li>Are we creating imagery or using provided assets?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>PART 9 — Recap &amp; Confirmation</h3>\n<p>You summarize:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary business goal</li>\n<li>Core problem to solve</li>\n<li>Target customer</li>\n<li>Key success metric</li>\n<li>Timeline</li>\n<li>Next steps</li>\n<li>Confirm alignment verbally.</li>\n<li>“Does this accurately reflect your vision and goals?”</li>\n<li>If yes — discovery complete.</li>\n</ul>"
        },
